%N A400002 Start with a set of two adjacent points in a hexagonal lattice. At each iteration, add to the set the lattice points which are adjacent to exactly two points already in the set, but only if those two points are adjacent to each other. The sequence gives the number of points in the set after n iterations.
%C A400002 Pattern resembles a periodic sequence of birds flying from the center in six directions with polymer-like chains emanating at an angle to the flight path. (see illustration for n = 100). Within each flight path, the birds seem to be produced every 34 iterations, beginning at n = 30 for the vertical paths and n = 45 for the diagonal paths. As for the chains, they appear to have a pattern that repeats every 10 iterations, and alternates every 5 iterations. See "Illustration of periodic patterns" under Links.
%C A400002 Starting with an odd number k of adjacent collinear points produces a sequence that terminates after k-1 iterations. For example, starting with five such points yields 5,13,23,27,33,33,33,... . If we start with any number k of adjacent collinear points, then for n = 0,1,...,k-1, the number of points after n iterations is k + n*(2*k-n+1) - 2*(n mod 2).
